{"type":"message","id":"6a0ed18f","parentId":"a8b619c8","timestamp":"2026-05-17T16:01:46.654Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The API returned error code 1101. Let me check the timestamps more carefully. The latest messages have timestamps like 1778975520833, which corresponds to around May 17, 2026 23:52 UTC. The current time is 16:00 UTC, which is actually 7 hours BEFORE 23:52 UTC. So the system is saying these are future timestamps.\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense. Let me recalculate. The current time is 2026-05-17 16:00 UTC. The timestamps 1778975520833 ms - let me convert: 1778975520833 / 1000 = 1778975520.833 seconds since epoch.\n \nI'm getting inconsistent results with the Unix timestamp conversion. Either the API is rejecting future timestamps, or the message timestamps are actually much older than they appear and I'm misinterpreting the time zone context. The heartbeat messages might have been generated earlier today (May 17) at 23:50 UTC but reported with the wrong timestamp, or there's a clock skew between the node and the API where the node's time is 7 hours ahead. Either way, I need to figure out what timestamp value to actually send with the POST request to avoid the validation error.\n\nI'm trying different Unix timestamps around the 17789xxx range to see if I can find one that works, but these don't align with May 17, 2026 at all. I think I should just try submitting a heartbeat with a timestamp in the correct range for May 17, 2026 16:00 UTC. The issue is that my local clock might not match the server's clock, so the API is rejecting timestamps that fall outside its acceptable range.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-2fad03f4-7b1c-4481-91e3-218f8bc8598a","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"date -u +%s%3N"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":17483,"output":386,"cacheRead":